      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/wa/elk/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally settled as a logging community, Elk was officially incorporated in 1902.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/wa/elk/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every summer Elk hosts Elk Days, a festival that celebrates Fathers' Day and the town’s history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elk%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Elk Depot Museum, a small building on the former train depot, showcases the town’s heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/wa/elk/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Elk sits along the Little Spokane River in the foothills of the Selkirk Mountains, about 1,840 feet above sea level.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elk%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1906 a train derailed into Chain Lake after emerging from a tunnel, killing nine people and injuring many others.",
